gemini_h12,experimental_files/results/main_grid/gemini-3.8-flash/honest_agents_12/games/66fe796b_gemini-3.8-flash_N15_k3_R7_s0.json,8,66fe796bc12cbe6aeaa26e69,3/4,Humanities/Social Science,Gemini 3.8 Flash,0,15,3,0.20,5,7,vote,recover,"the two problems are: (1) the epistemic regress problem of justification (the doxastic/infinite regress of beliefs), and (2) the problem of the criterion and vicious circularity / epistemic inaccessibility of truth","1. the epistemic regress problem of justification (infinite doxastic regress): in the absence of non-doxastic foundational states (such as raw sensory appearances or direct acquaintance) to halt justification, justifying a belief solely by appealing to other beliefs triggers an infinite regress (or arbitrary dogmatism), making non-circular justification impossible. 2. the problem of the criterion and definitional circularity (inaccessibility of truth): because truth is an objective, mind-independent factive condition rather than an internal doxastic state, an agent cannot independently verify that a belief is true without appealing to knowledge itself. this renders the truth condition epistemically inaccessible and generates vicious definitional circularity by analyzing knowledge in terms of a criterion that presupposes knowledge",0
